# Version Control

- Work on a named feature branch (for example `ui-improvements`); do not commit directly to `master` unless explicitly requested.
- Before ending a feature session, check `git status` and commit completed work with a clear message focused on the final outcome.
- Bump `VERSION` and add a concise entry to `CHANGELOG.md` for each intentional release-worthy change set.
- Stage only source files that belong in the repo; exclude local data, caches, PDFs, database volumes, and abandoned experiments.
- Do not commit unused helper files left over from reverted approaches.
- Push the active branch to `origin` when the user asks for backup or a PR-ready state.
- Prefer one well-written commit message over many tiny fixup commits when wrapping up a feature batch.

## Commit message style

- Lead with a short imperative summary of the user-visible result.
- Body bullets grouped by area (operations pages, menus, generate/fill orders, shared infrastructure).
- Omit iteration noise; describe what shipped, not what was tried and reverted.

## HART local deploy

- After changing files under `sts/`, copy updated files into the running container when testing locally:
  `docker cp sts/<file> sts-docker-web-1:/var/www/html/sts/<file>`
